Data Quality Methodology

Purpose

The workflow checks whether financial totals reconcile for each processed record. It identifies missing or invalid numeric values and differences that exceed the configured tolerance.

Financial Reconciliation Rule

The workflow calculates:

Difference = Gross Cost - Earned Revenue - Net Cost

All amounts are expressed in billions of US dollars.

Validation Rules

1. Missing or Invalid Values

The workflow checks whether gross cost, earned revenue, or net cost is missing, empty, or not a valid finite number.

If any value fails this check, the record receives the status:

MISSING_OR_INVALID_DATA

2. Reconciliation Difference

When all three values are valid, the workflow calculates the difference and rounds it to two decimal places.

The configured tolerance is 0.1 billion.

OK: The absolute difference is less than or equal to 0.1 billion.

REVIEW_REQUIRED: The absolute difference exceeds 0.1 billion.

MISSING_OR_INVALID_DATA: One or more required values are missing or invalid.

Summary Metrics

The workflow produces these summary fields:

total_records_checked — Number of records evaluated.

records_passed — Number of records with status OK.

records_flagged — Number of records that did not receive status OK.

data_quality_status — PASS when no records are flagged; otherwise REVIEW REQUIRED.

flagged_records — Details of records requiring further review, including agency, fiscal year, difference, and validation message.

Interpreting Flags

A flagged record is an investigation prompt, not proof of an error. Differences can arise from source-data issues, rounding, definitions, reporting conventions, or other accounting considerations. The workflow does not independently determine which explanation applies.

Review flagged records against the authoritative source and relevant financial reporting documentation before drawing conclusions.

Scope and Limitations

These checks are basic arithmetic reconciliation controls. They do not constitute a complete financial audit, establish the accuracy of source records, or verify compliance with accounting standards.

Results depend on the records retrieved from NocoDB and the fields supplied to the validation nodes.

Example

Suppose a record contains:

Gross cost: 100.0 billion

Earned revenue: 20.0 billion

Net cost: 79.8 billion

The calculated difference is:

100.0 - 20.0 - 79.8 = 0.2 billion

Since the absolute difference exceeds the configured tolerance of 0.1 billion, the record receives REVIEW_REQUIRED.

Disclaimer

This methodology documents automated checks in a portfolio project. It is not an official US Treasury audit methodology or an assurance opinion.
